WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:00.811
One prompt.

00:01.488 --> 00:02.570
Five hidden jobs.

00:03.237 --> 00:04.567
And an answer you can't check.

00:05.183 --> 00:07.610
That's what happens when you ask Claude to

00:07.610 --> 00:10.152
analyse a quarter of market data and propose

00:10.152 --> 00:11.654
strategies, all in one go.

00:12.254 --> 00:13.308
Here's where we're heading:

00:13.963 --> 00:16.667
the same request as five small steps, each one

00:16.667 --> 00:17.255
checkable.

00:17.849 --> 00:20.290
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able to

00:20.290 --> 00:22.842
break any big business request into a chain of

00:22.842 --> 00:25.395
steps that Claude gets right, and that you can

00:25.395 --> 00:25.783
verify.

00:26.383 --> 00:27.119
Chapter one.

00:27.836 --> 00:29.137
Spot the hidden jobs.

00:29.737 --> 00:31.539
Here's a prompt many of us have typed:

00:32.162 --> 00:35.180
analyse our Q3 market data and propose five

00:35.180 --> 00:35.952
strategies.

00:36.514 --> 00:38.845
It looks like one request, but it hides at

00:38.845 --> 00:39.733
least five jobs:

00:40.315 --> 00:42.798
read the data, spot the trends, explain the

00:42.798 --> 00:44.992
causes, generate ideas, and rank them.

00:45.574 --> 00:48.076
Asked in one breath, the model has to juggle

00:48.076 --> 00:49.213
all of them at once.

00:49.788 --> 00:52.108
The usual result is generic strategies,

00:52.108 --> 00:54.727
skipped steps, or numbers that were never in

00:54.727 --> 00:55.262
the data.

00:55.862 --> 00:58.093
Chain of thought means asking the model to

00:58.093 --> 01:00.376
write out its reasoning before it gives the

01:00.376 --> 01:00.748
answer.

01:01.341 --> 01:03.724
Each step is on the page, so later steps can

01:03.724 --> 01:06.052
build on earlier ones, and you can read the

01:06.052 --> 01:08.489
reasoning to see exactly where it went wrong.

01:09.059 --> 01:11.752
Same model, same data, but more of the work

01:11.752 --> 01:14.446
happens out loud, which usually means fewer

01:14.446 --> 01:16.325
errors on multi-step problems.

01:16.925 --> 01:19.133
Task decomposition works one level up.

01:19.714 --> 01:22.169
Instead of one prompt doing everything, you

01:22.169 --> 01:24.281
break the project into small, ordered

01:24.281 --> 01:24.852
sub-tasks.

01:25.421 --> 01:28.005
Each one has a clear input, a single job, and

01:28.005 --> 01:28.867
a clear output.

01:29.434 --> 01:31.934
Chain of thought improves the reasoning inside

01:31.934 --> 01:32.314
a step.

01:32.941 --> 01:35.520
Decomposition improves the structure between

01:35.520 --> 01:35.872
steps.

01:36.424 --> 01:37.372
You'll want both.

01:37.972 --> 01:38.586
Takeaway:

01:39.327 --> 01:42.148
if a request hides several jobs, give each job

01:42.148 --> 01:42.945
its own step.

01:43.545 --> 01:44.318
Chapter two.

01:45.003 --> 01:46.576
Turn one prompt into five.

01:47.176 --> 01:48.408
Here's the before version:

01:49.053 --> 01:51.764
one prompt, the Q3 data pasted in, and a

01:51.764 --> 01:53.661
request for five strategies.

01:54.253 --> 01:55.253
You do get an answer.

01:55.908 --> 01:58.461
But you can't tell which numbers came from the

01:58.461 --> 02:01.014
data, why these five were chosen, or where the

02:01.014 --> 02:01.902
reasoning broke.

02:02.502 --> 02:03.712
Now the after version.

02:04.353 --> 02:04.976
Step one:

02:05.672 --> 02:08.011
extract the key metrics, and quote the source

02:08.011 --> 02:08.271
rows.

02:08.847 --> 02:09.530
Step two:

02:10.247 --> 02:11.719
find the three biggest trends.

02:12.292 --> 02:12.997
Step three:

02:13.676 --> 02:15.667
explain the likely cause of each trend.

02:16.284 --> 02:16.977
Step four:

02:17.685 --> 02:20.446
brainstorm ten strategy options tied to those

02:20.446 --> 02:20.876
causes.

02:21.444 --> 02:22.173
Step five:

02:22.842 --> 02:25.326
score them on impact and effort, and pick the

02:25.326 --> 02:25.878
best five.

02:26.456 --> 02:28.807
Every output becomes the next step's input.

02:29.407 --> 02:30.450
Phrasing matters.

02:31.101 --> 02:33.361
Plain, think step by step, helps a little.

02:33.980 --> 02:35.559
Better is to spell out the steps:

02:36.201 --> 02:38.337
first do this, then that, then the next thing.

02:39.031 --> 02:40.380
Best for review is structure.

02:41.049 --> 02:43.484
Ask Claude to put its reasoning inside

02:43.484 --> 02:46.112
thinking tags and the final result inside

02:46.112 --> 02:46.881
answer tags.

02:47.444 --> 02:49.765
Now you can read the work, and your code or a

02:49.765 --> 02:51.776
colleague can pull out just the answer.

02:52.376 --> 02:54.971
For bigger projects, run each step as its own

02:54.971 --> 02:56.989
prompt and pass the output forward.

02:57.603 --> 02:58.870
That's called prompt chaining.

02:59.501 --> 03:02.138
Between steps you can check the result, fix a

03:02.138 --> 03:04.365
mistake early, or rerun just one step.

03:04.931 --> 03:07.420
If step two found the wrong trends, you fix

03:07.420 --> 03:09.619
step two, not the whole strategy deck.

03:10.219 --> 03:10.833
Takeaway:

03:11.599 --> 03:13.878
make the reasoning visible, and check each

03:13.878 --> 03:15.832
output before the next step uses it.

03:16.432 --> 03:17.243
Chapter three.

03:17.936 --> 03:19.293
Decompose it yourself.

03:19.893 --> 03:20.981
Pause the video now.

03:21.618 --> 03:22.576
Take this task:

03:23.225 --> 03:25.368
plan the launch of a new product next quarter.

03:26.026 --> 03:27.560
Write four to six sub-tasks.

03:28.180 --> 03:30.737
For each one, note its input, its output, and

03:30.737 --> 03:32.840
how you'd check that output is right.

03:33.428 --> 03:35.702
Then write the prompt for step one, using

03:35.702 --> 03:37.422
explicit step-by-step phrasing.

03:38.016 --> 03:40.193
Give yourself five minutes, then press play.

03:40.793 --> 03:42.312
Here's one possible answer.

03:42.975 --> 03:44.819
Define the audience and the launch goal.

03:45.473 --> 03:46.619
Scan the competitors.

03:47.244 --> 03:49.280
Write the positioning and the key message.

03:49.849 --> 03:51.596
Choose channels and split the budget.

03:52.246 --> 03:54.346
Then build the timeline and list the risks.

03:54.903 --> 03:56.976
Yours may look different, and that's fine.

03:57.575 --> 03:58.189
Takeaway:

03:58.938 --> 04:01.754
every step gets one job, and an output you can

04:01.754 --> 04:02.121
check.

04:02.721 --> 04:03.512
Chapter four.

04:04.239 --> 04:05.432
Know when to use it.

04:06.033 --> 04:08.736
Decompose when the task has several stages,

04:08.736 --> 04:11.061
involves numbers, drives an important

04:11.061 --> 04:13.826
decision, or when someone needs to audit the

04:13.826 --> 04:14.455
reasoning.

04:15.038 --> 04:17.694
Keep it to one prompt for simple lookups and

04:17.694 --> 04:20.230
quick rewrites, where extra steps only add

04:20.230 --> 04:21.075
time and cost.

04:21.675 --> 04:22.289
Takeaway:

04:23.067 --> 04:25.639
decompose when the work has stages, numbers,

04:25.639 --> 04:26.223
or stakes.

04:26.834 --> 04:28.250
Otherwise, keep it simple.

04:28.850 --> 04:29.675
Let's recap.

04:30.372 --> 04:31.945
Big prompts hide many jobs.

04:32.545 --> 04:34.981
Chain of thought and explicit phrasing make

04:34.981 --> 04:36.001
the steps visible.

04:36.601 --> 04:39.437
Each step gets one job, a clear input, and an

04:39.437 --> 04:40.761
output you can check.

04:41.361 --> 04:43.455
And you decompose when the work is

04:43.455 --> 04:45.734
multi-stage, numeric, or high-stakes.
